1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
One sheet of paper, 6 cm long and 1 cm wide. Three different questions can be asked of it.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
How much fence goes round the edge? That is the perimeter: 6 + 1 + 6 + 1 = 14 cm.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
How much paint covers it? That is the area: 6 times 1 = 6 square centimetres.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
How much water fits inside? That is a volume, and a flat sheet holds none until it is folded into a solid.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
A square 1 cm on each side has area 1 square centimetre. Six of them tile the sheet exactly.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
Area is a count of unit squares, which is why it is written in square centimetres and not in centimetres.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
A cube 1 cm on each side has volume 1 cubic centimetre: 1 times 1 times 1.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
The sheet has area 6, yet a square of side 2 does not fit in it: 2 by 2 needs 4 square centimetres and a width of 2 cm.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Double the side of a square and the area is not doubled. It is four times as large: 1 becomes 4.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Double the side of a cube and the volume is eight times: 1 becomes 8. Lengths go k, areas k squared, volumes k cubed.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
With k = 3: a length triples, an area is nine times as large, a volume twenty-seven times.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So a model at one tenth scale needs one hundredth of the paint and holds one thousandth of the water.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Roll the sheet by bringing its two short edges together. The long edge, 6 cm, closes into a circle.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
That circle is the base circumference: 2 pi r = 6, so r = 3/pi, about 0.955 cm.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The short edge, 1 cm, becomes the height. It does not change at all.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The paper still has area 6: the curved surface is 2 pi r h = 2 pi times 3/pi times 1 = 6.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
A cylinder is a disc stacked: base area pi r squared times height. The disc is pi times 9/pi squared, which is 9/pi, about 2.865.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
The height is 1, so the volume is 9/pi, about 2.865 cubic centimetres.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The other sheet is cut into six squares of side 1 and folded into a closed cube: side 1, volume 1.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
The ratio is 9/pi, about 2.865, which is the recorded answer to the GATE 2021 question.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A closed cube has six identical square faces, so its surface is 6 a squared. Setting 6 a squared = 6 gives a = 1.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
A 6 by 1 strip cuts exactly into six 1 by 1 squares, so a = 1 is the largest cube the sheet allows.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
From the same 6 square centimetres, the cube holds 1 and the cylinder holds 2.865.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The cylinder wins because its paper is only the curved side. The ends are assumed closed and no paper pays for them.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Roll by bringing the long edges together and the circle has circumference 1: r = 1 over 2 pi, about 0.159 cm.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
The height is now 6, and the volume is pi r squared h = 3/(2 pi), about 0.477, six times smaller.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Same paper, same area, six times less water: which edge becomes the circle decides the answer.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
The 2021 question says by bringing the short edges together, so the long edge is the circle. That phrase is the whole question.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A triangle is half a rectangle: half base times height. Base 4, height 3, area 6.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Turn it: base 5, and the height to that base is 12/5 = 2.4. Half of 5 times 2.4 is still 6.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Double every side and the area is four times as large: 3-4-5 becomes 6-8-10, area 24, not 12.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Similar figures have areas in the ratio k squared and volumes in the ratio k cubed: the same scale law as the square and the cube.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The 2022 question gives a curve that is 2, then 3, then 1, each flat across its interval.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
Each piece is a rectangle. The widths are 1/3, 5/12 and 1/4, and they add to 1.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
The products are 2/3, 5/4 and 1/4, and the sum is 13/6, about 2.167: option C.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Taking 3/4 minus 1/3 as 1/4, or using the tallest height 3 everywhere, produces the wrong options.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
With 12 cm of fence the integer rectangles are 1 by 5, 2 by 4 and 3 by 3: areas 5, 8 and 9. The square wins.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
With 6 square cm of paper closed around the shape, a cube holds 1 and a sphere holds about 1.382.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Neither answer needs calculus: enumerate the few cases, or quote the standard optimum and state its condition.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Four habits: name the quantity, fix the unit, decide which length is which, and check the scale law.

